INT. NIGHT TRAIN - SLEEPER COMPARTMENT - NIGHT

A narrow cabin of dark wood and brass. Two bunks. A lamp swings with the rails. Rain slides sideways down the window.

CLEMENCE ARDOIN (30s, wool coat, hair undone) sits on the lower bunk with her shoes still on. SULEIMAN HADDAD (30s, travel-worn suit, a single suitcase) stands at the window with his back to her. He does not turn.

She watches the back of his neck. He watches the dark.

CLEMENCE
You could have taken another car.

SULEIMAN
There was no other car.

CLEMENCE
There's always another car.

SULEIMAN
Not at two in the morning.

CLEMENCE
You didn't ask.

SULEIMAN
Neither did you.

The rails clack. She smooths her skirt. He loosens his tie, pulls it off, folds it once, twice, and tucks it in his pocket.

CLEMENCE
Tell me why you came.

SULEIMAN
I told you.

CLEMENCE
You told me a story about a conference.

SULEIMAN
It was a good conference.

CLEMENCE
In Lyon. Where I live.

SULEIMAN
It's a big city.

CLEMENCE
Suleiman.

SULEIMAN
Clemence.

CLEMENCE
Say it.

SULEIMAN
I got on the wrong train.

CLEMENCE
You got on the right one.

SULEIMAN
That's worse.

CLEMENCE
Yes.

He finally turns. The lamp lights half his face. She is already standing. They are closer than either planned.

SULEIMAN
Six years.

CLEMENCE
Seven in March.

SULEIMAN
You counted.

CLEMENCE
You didn't?

SULEIMAN
Every day.

CLEMENCE
Then why is this the first time?

SULEIMAN
Because you would have said no.

CLEMENCE
I might have said yes.

SULEIMAN
Might.

CLEMENCE
Might.

The train screams around a curve and throws her against his chest. He catches her. Neither lets go.

CLEMENCE (CONT'D)
That was an accident.

SULEIMAN
I know.

CLEMENCE
I'm not sorry.

SULEIMAN
Neither am I.

She slaps him, hard, across the cheek. He doesn't flinch. She slaps him again, and then she is crying, and then she is holding onto his lapels, and he says her name into her hair, again and again, as the rain beats on the glass and the train carries them toward a station neither of them knows.
